---

<header class="global-nav">
    <div class="nav-content">
        <button
            class="nav-btn menu-btn"
            id="menuToggle"
            aria-label="Abrir menú de navegación"
        >
            <span class="menu-symbol">π</span>
        </button>

        <a href="/" class="nav-logo" aria-label="Agorá Academy">
            <!-- Inlining SVG to ensure we can control it if needed, or just img -->
            <img src="/favicon/favicon.svg" alt="Agorá Academy" />
        </a>

        <button
            class="nav-btn theme-btn"
            id="themeToggle"
            aria-label="Cambiar entre tema claro y oscuro"
        >
            <span id="themeIcon" aria-hidden="true">🌑</span>
            <span class="sr-only" id="themeText">Cambiar a tema oscuro</span>
        </button>
    </div>
</header>
<script>
    import "../scripts/theme.js";
    // We don't import menu.js here because Menu.astro handles it (and the overlay)
    // But we need to make sure Menu.astro is present in the layout.
</script>

<style>
    .global-nav {
        position: fixed;
        top: 0;
        left: 0;
        right: 0;
        height: 48px;
        z-index: 2000; /* Higher than menu overlay? No, menu overlay is 1000. 
                     Wait, if overlay is screen covering, nav should be on top? 
                     Typically nav is on top of content, but menu overlay covers EVERYTHING including nav. 
                     Apple's menu usually covers the nav or transforms usage.
                     Let's verify Menu.astro z-index. It was 1000. 
                     If I put nav at 2000, it stays on top of menu overlay.
                     If I want standard drawer behavior, overlay is usually ON TOP of everything.
                     Let's check Menu.astro styles again. 
                     Button was 1001. Overlay 1000. 
                     So button remains clickable? 
                     Yes, button was fixed. 
                     So nav should be slightly lower than overlay if overlay covers header, 
                     OR nav stays on top if the "close" button is inside the nav. 
                     Current Menu.astro overlay covers entire screen (top:0).
                     And standard apple menu behavior: The header transforms into the close button.
                     Let's keep nav at z-index 1001 (like the old button) so it's visible. 
                  */
        background-color: rgba(
            22,
            22,
            24,
            0.8
        ); /* Apple-like dark semi-transparent */
        backdrop-filter: saturate(180%) blur(20px);
        -webkit-backdrop-filter: saturate(180%) blur(20px);
        border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
        transition: background-color 0.3s ease;
    }

    /* Light mode override for nav background if desired, but Apple often keeps dark header or adaptive. 
     Let's handle light mode via CSS var if we have them. 
     Global CSS likely implies variables. 
     But for "Apple Style", it's often consistent or dark. 
     Let's stick to dark glass for now, or use variables if available.
  */

    :global([data-theme="light"]) .global-nav {
        background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8);
        border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
    }

    .nav-content {
        max-width: 1000px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        height: 100%;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        padding: 0 20px;
    }

    .nav-btn {
        background: transparent;
        border: none;
        cursor: pointer;
        color: #f5f5f7; /* Apple light grey text */
        padding: 0;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        width: 40px;
        height: 100%;
        transition: opacity 0.3s;
    }

    :global([data-theme="light"]) .nav-btn {
        color: #1d1d1f;
    }

    .nav-btn:hover {
        opacity: 0.7;
    }

    .menu-symbol,
    #themeIcon {
        font-size: 1.2rem;
        line-height: 1;
    }

    .menu-symbol {
        font-size: 1.5rem; /* Make Pi bigger */
    }

    .nav-logo {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: center;
        height: 100%;
        opacity: 0.9;
        transition: opacity 0.3s;
    }

    .nav-logo:hover {
        opacity: 1;
    }

    .nav-logo img {
        height: 18px; /* Apple logo is usually small ~16-20px */
        width: auto;
        display: block;
    }

    /* Adjust body padding to prevent content hiding behind fixed header */
    /* This needs to be global or applied in layout. */

    @media (max-width: 768px) {
        .nav-content {
            padding: 0 16px;
        }
    }
</style>
